      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.4.06-03",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Coal generation can provide controllable output when fuel, machinery and network capacity are available. Stockpiles can buffer some delivery variation. However, mining and transport affect land and communities, and ash needs management. Burning coal releases carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas that adds to the enhanced greenhouse effect and global warming, and sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, which form acid rain that damages soils, forests, lakes and fish. Smoke and dust also reduce air quality. Pollution controls can reduce particular emissions without making combustion carbon-free. Continuing fuel and maintenance costs must be included in an affordability judgement alongside initial construction.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.4.06-1",
        "CL-6137-2.4.06-2",
