    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.4.06-05",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Nuclear disadvantages include high initial capital requirements, long planning or construction periods in many projects, radioactive waste management and decommissioning responsibilities. Severe accidents are rare but serious. On 11 March 2011 an earthquake and tsunami cut the power and cooling at Japan's Fukushima Daiichi station; three reactor cores melted, radioactive material escaped and about 110,000 people were evacuated because of radiation. A comparison should not call nuclear renewable because it has low direct carbon emissions, or call it fossil because its fuel is mined. Replenishment, emissions and risk are separate dimensions.",
